 only a few of the clinical measurements needed to tell the full story of their health. PULSE is a new artificial intelligence (AI) framework that fills in the blanks, using a patient’s own history to predict what was never directly measured.\nL42: \nL43:     * Stephanie Tanis\nL44:     * Samantha Lopez Alvarez\nL45:     * Natalie R. Davidson\nL46: \nL47: News & Views 26 Aug 2026 cite9†Nature Computational Science L52: Reliably linking patient-level cancer phenotypes to single-cell transcriptomes re-mains challenging due to limited cell-level labels. Here, the authors develop scTransMIL, a transformer-based multi-instance learning framework that identifies cancer states, cancer subtypes, and potential cancer biomarkers with good performance across datasets.\nL53: \nL54:     * Zhenchao Tang\nL55:     * Fang Wang\nL56:     * Jianhua Yao\nL57: \nL58: Research Open Access 14 Sept 2026 cite16†Nature Communications L59:   * ### cite17†A clinically-oriented foundation model for intraoperative pathology L60: \nL61: CRISP, a vision-based pathology foundation model developed exclusively from frozen section slides, supports treatment decision-making throughout the surgical workflow with superior performance to current foundation models and extensive validation, including in a prospective cohort.\nL62: \nL63:     * Zihan Zhao\nL64:     * Fengtao Zhou\nL65:     * Muyan Cai\nL66: \nL67: Research Open Access 10 Sept 2026 cite18†Nature Medicine L68: \nL69: P: 1\nL70:   * cite19†Image†media.springernature.com L71: ### cite20†Segment anything in pathology images with natural language L72: \nL73: This Resource introduces PathSegmentor—a foundation model that segments 160 pathological categories via natural language prompts, generalizes across datasets and clinical cohorts, and enables scalable, interpretable pathology image analysis.\nL74: \nL75:     * Zhixuan Chen\nL76:     * Junlin Hou\nL77:     * Hao Chen\nL78: \nL79: Research 10 Sept 2026 cite9†Nature Computational Science L80: \nL81: P: 1-15\nL82:   * ### cite21†Explainable data generation from observed small samples by matrix tri-factorization L83: \nL84: Here the authors propose an explainable data generation approach called EDG that operates under extreme sample scarcity by exploiting inherent low-dimensional principles contained in the observed high-dimensional data via matrix tri-factorization.\nL85: \nL86:     * Bin Zhang\nL87:     * Peng Tao\nL88:     * Luonan Chen\nL89: \nL90: Research Open Access 09 Sept 2026 cite16†Nature Communications L91:   * ### cite22†Reducing cost of fluorescent microscopy through dye exclusion, distillation techniques, and deep learning L92: \nL93:     * Adriana Borowa\nL94:     * Bartosz Zieliński\nL95:     * Dawid Rymarczyk\nL96: \nL97: Research Open Access 09 Sept 2026 cite23†Scientific Reports L98: \nL99:   * cite24†Image†media.springernature.com L100: ### cite25†Breaking timescales with generative sampling of conformational transitions L101: \nL102: A generative committor-guided path-sampling framework reconstructs rare biomolecular transition pathways and reveals the underlying thermodynamics and kinetics without using predefined collective variables or brute-force sampling, at an acceptable computational cost.\nL103: \nL104:     * Chenyu Tang\nL105:     * Mayank Prakash Pandey\nL106:     * Christophe Chipot\nL107: \nL108: Research 09 Sept 2026 cite26†Nature L109: \nL110: P: 1-6\nL111: \nL112: cite27†All Research & Reviews L113: ## News and Comment\nL114: \nL115:   * cite28†Image†media.springernature.com L116: \nL117: ### cite29†‘Multifunctional’ brain implant translates speech and gestures in real time L118: \nL119: A neural device uses artificial intelligence to read brain activity for intended words and gestures simultaneously.\nL120: \nL121:     * Miryam Naddaf\nL122: \nL123: News 14 Sept 2026 cite26†Nature L124: \nL125:   * cite30†Image†media.springernature.com L126: ### cite31†Drug firms’ secret data supercharge AI protein models L127: \nL128: An AI system trained on more than 20,000 protein structures from pharmaceutical companies outperforms AlphaFold-like models that use only public data.\nL129: \nL130:     * Ewen Callaway\nL131: \nL132: News 14 Sept 2026 cite26†Nature L133: \nL134:   * cite32†Image†media.springernature.com L135: ### cite33†Briefing Chat: The Bunsen burner myth that turns out to be just hot air L136: \nL137: Nature staff discuss an AI tool that can assess the impact of 9 billion mutations — plus, how the Bunsen burner’s reputation for sterilization might be based on tradition alone.\nL138: \nL139:     * Shamini Bundell\nL140:     * Nick Petrić Howe\nL141: \nL142: News 11 Sept 2026 cite26†Nature L143: \nL144:   * cite7†Image†media.springernature.com L145: ### cite8†When pathology segmentation learns to listen L146: \nL147: A natural-language-guided pathology segmentation model is developed to link pathological language with pathology image content to produce semantic masks, offering a path toward computational pathology systems that are more flexible, interpretable and aligned with human expertise.\nL148: \nL149:     * Wei Shen\nL150: \nL151: News & Views 10 Sept 2026 cite9†Nature Computational Science L152: \nL153: P: 1-2\nL154: \nL155:   * cite34†Image†media.springernature.com L156: ### cite35†Use AI as a sparring partner, not an oracle L157: \nL158: As artificial-intelligence tools become crucial collaborators in science, universities must teach students to challenge such systems and assess their ability to do so.\nL159: \nL160:     * Marc Schmitt\nL161: \nL162: Comments & Opinion 09 Sept 2026 cite26†Nature L163: \nL164: Volume: 657, P: 575\nL165: \nL166:   * cite36†Image†media.springernature.com L167: ### cite37†AI model predicts which breast-cancer drugs work best L168: \nL169: Model trained on millions of protein measurements can gauge drug effectiveness in tissue samples taken from peop